## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread that exceeds a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006).

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout; throws if cancelled
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(work, cts.Token).WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Best: when work itself cooperates with cancellation
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(token), token).WaitAsync(token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only works if `work` checks the token. If `work` is a tight CPU loop that never yields, it won't stop until it finishes. |
| **Fire-and-forget work leaks** | `Task.Run(work)` continues running even after `WaitAsync` throws. The work isn't killed — it's abandoned. This is the fundamental tradeoff vs. the old `Thread.Abort`. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always wrap in `using` — it holds timer resources internally. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use the `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to distinguish your timeout from external cancellation. |

### For Truly Uncooperative Code

If `work` calls third-party code that can't check a token and **must** be terminated:

```csharp
// Last resort: run in a child process and use Process.Kill()
public T ExecuteWithHardTimeout<T>(string workerExePath, string args, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var process = Process.Start(workerExePath, args);
    if (!process.WaitForExit(timeout))
    {
        process.Kill(entireProcessTree: true);
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
    // Deserialize result from process stdout
}
```

### What Was Removed

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `cts.CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested` |
| Manual `Thread` + `Join` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync` |

### Summary

The paradigm shift is from **preemptive** (runtime injects exception) to **cooperative** (code must voluntarily check). For best results, pass `CancellationToken` into `work` and call `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at regular checkpoints inside long-running operations.
